---
import { getEntry } from 'astro:content';
import LegalPage from '../../components/LegalPage.astro';
import Button from '../../components/Button.astro';
import GuideFigure from '../../components/GuideFigure.astro';

const template = await getEntry('templates', 'ethan-walker');
if (!template) throw new Error('The portfolio guide needs the ethan-walker template.');
const t = template.data;
const title = 'How to build a portfolio with a Framer template';
const description = 'Set up a designer portfolio in Framer with Ethan Walker: what to prepare, how to add projects in the CMS and what to check before you publish.';
const url = new URL('/guides/framer-portfolio-template/', Astro.site).href;
---

<LegalPage accent="portfolio"
  kicker="Guide"
  title={title}
  seoTitle="Framer portfolio template: how to build your portfolio"
  description={description}
  updated="November 3, 2026"
  jsonLd={{
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'Article',
    headline: title,
    description,
    url,
    mainEntityOfPage: url,
    dateModified: '2026-11-03',
    author: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'Vonex Design', url: new URL('/', Astro.site).href },
  }}
>
  <p>
    A portfolio works best when it shows a few strong projects and is easy to update. This guide uses
    <a href={`/templates/${template.id}/`}>{t.name}</a>, a free Framer portfolio template, as the example. The steps
    also fit any other template with a CMS.
  </p>

  <GuideFigure template={template} caption="Ethan Walker, the template used in this guide" />

  <h2>What the template includes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Pages: Home, About, Work, a Project page for each case study, Contact, Privacy Policy and a 404 page.</li>
    <li>CMS collections: Projects, Services and the Privacy Policy.</li>
    <li>A contact form, scroll and hover effects, and large typography.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>1. Choose the work to show</h2>
  <p>
    Pick three to six projects. For each one, write what the problem was, what you did and what changed. Add two to four
    images. Fewer, better projects beat a long list.
  </p>

  <h2>2. Copy the template</h2>
  <p>
    Open <a href={`/templates/${template.id}/`}>{t.name}</a>, press "View on Framer", then remix it into your account.
    The general steps are in <a href="/guides/how-to-use-framer-templates/">how to use a Framer template</a>.
    Still choosing? Compare <a href="/guides/free-framer-portfolio-templates/">the best free Framer portfolio templates</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>3. Add your projects in the CMS</h2>
  <p>
    The Work page and the project pages are driven by the Projects collection. Open the CMS in Framer, replace the sample
    items with your own and fill every field: title, cover, text and images. Each item becomes its own project page.
    You do not edit the design to add a project. More on how collections work in <a href="/guides/framer-cms/">the Framer CMS guide</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Do the same for Services if you offer them. Delete sample items you do not need, so no placeholder reaches the
    live site.
  </p>

  <h2>4. Rewrite the About and Home pages</h2>
  <p>
    Say who you are, what you do and who you work with, in a few plain sentences. Put your best project and a clear
    contact link near the top.
  </p>

  <h2>5. Set up the contact form</h2>
  <p>
    Point the form to your own email, send a test message and check that it arrives. Keep your email address visible
    as a fallback.
  </p>

  <h2>6. Check and publish</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>View every page on desktop, tablet and phone.</li>
    <li>Write a title and description for each page. Add a social preview image.</li>
    <li>Update the Privacy Policy item so it describes your own site.</li>
    <li>Check your Framer plan for CMS and page limits, and whether you need a custom domain. See <a href="/guides/how-to-use-framer-templates/">what it costs</a>.</li>
    <li>Publish, then open the live site and click through it once more.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Keep it current</h2>
  <p>
    Add a new project to the CMS when you finish one. A portfolio that was updated this month looks alive. Remove old
    work that no longer represents you.
  </p>

  <div class="mt-xl flex flex-wrap gap-sm">
    <Button href={`/templates/${template.id}/`} arrow>See {t.name}</Button>
    <Button href="/templates/" variant="secondary" arrow>Browse templates</Button>
  </div>
</LegalPage>
